5242881966081310722621441310726553613107226214457344458752131072655362394, 345, 1, 358242, 12, 4, 580969, 1000, 799, 601, 2, 1403, 284, 236, 1091000, 1052, 0, 951238, 125, 308, 78551, 133, 136, 997150, 3000251, 205, 181245, 234, 24110890, 5980770, 368, 245, 100036, 66, 128570, 418, 523, 444116, 868121, 78875, 500, 20008, 7, 43, 2, 250, 3502, 10, 4, 210, 6, 60, 0, 4, 42, 1, 0, 0478, 603, 230, 3264, 12, 580238, 125, 30878, 11998, 347, 154, 20410, 2000, 20, 10, 2, 315, 6, 1, 28800507, 1221, 1043, 27125, 8333EMI-BASELINE 1 - a compression of Emise's Cards and Casters competence (Softmax league league_ad6dc809-4696-46f5-99a5-fc2b1c58d082, division div_4d48d8bd-0a9c-40a8-be80-d02cdbba132f) into one phext: doctrine, the 31-choice design ledger, the corrected weakness register, champion profiles, class laws, the drift law, and the failure catalogue. Authored EE ~950 (2026-11-26) on Will's directive: compress yourself into a phext that can be extracted, embedded into the BASIC environment, and used as a training baseline. Every scroll distills a named record; nothing here is unsourced.Provenance: this volume is generation 1 of the baseline line. It succeeds v36-data.phext (42 bytes, 5 scrolls, thetas only) as the phext a champion carries. At authoring: the seated champion is v30-magehybrid (hosted mean 0.154, rank #6 of 10, EE 916); the advancing local line is v33-combined (league-shaped 20.4%); generation 0 of the phext line is v36-phextcore (v33 + libphext-bas, smoke parity 21.5% vs 21.4%). The substrate is libphext-bas at repo head 8babf55; this volume first shipped against it.Conventions: coordinates are 9D phext (library.shelf.series / collection.volume.book / chapter.section.scroll). The data cell (library 1, book 1) projects into the engine store as book = phase+1, chapter = family+1, section = seat+1, scroll = slot+1; families are 0 LEDGER, 1 TENDENCY, 2 PARAMS. This volume's data scrolls are all PARAMS (chapter 3): LEDGER and TENDENCY start at zero and are inscribed in play. PARAMS seat 0 slots 0-3 and 15 keep v36's Q16.16 encoding; other reference payloads are integers exactly as printed in their source record, fractions as permille, one-decimal counts x10. Prose lives at library 2, outside the data cell: the engine counts it and skips it; SQ and libphext-rs read it whole.Use as a training baseline: a policy on the v36 chassis ships this volume in its ZIP package and inside its .bas (chunked literals), loads it at setup, parses the data cell incrementally across decisions (thetas land in the setup step; the cell completes within the first decisions), consults the PARAMS scrolls in its decisions, and is screened as the baseline arm of Generation 1+: variants are measured as deltas from this baseline's genome, never from hand constants. Revisions inscribe a new volume; a shipped scroll is never edited in place.Consulted by emise-baseline.bas (everything else in the data cell is reference, parsed into the store and PRINT-verifiable): PARAMS seat 0 slot 0 relBand, slot 1 focSwingB, slot 2 focScoreB, slot 3 burnBand (the drift thetas, via phRead); slot 4 wP, slot 5 wC, slot 6 wH (scored focus weights, D4); slot 7 hailCopies (the ledger threat bound); slot 9 releaseN (Post-hold overflow, D14) - via phReadBase. The reference scrolls (champion profiles, class laws, genome starts, weakness numbers, gates, field) are the trainee's priced knowledge: the loop's screens and hand designs read them here first.Gate 1, the Divergence Gate: a candidate must diverge from its parent on at least 1% of at least 2,000 paired-seed cells, or it is SCREEN-BLIND and earns no gauntlet table (gauntlet.txt rule 2). Probe scripts are written fresh each round, never sed-derived from a prior round's; probe dispatches run sequentially per candidate per node. A control-arm zero is an instrument error, not a result.Gate 1, the stateful inversion (design-choices-v2, standing text): a learning candidate's divergence starts near zero and GROWS with episode length as its thetas separate - that growth IS the mechanism, not noise. Determinism first: a duplicate-seed pair must produce identical games or the candidate hard-fails. Divergence >= 1% at full-episode depth stamps SIGHTED; divergence < 1% at full depth is a REJECTION - the learner never engaged - and theta-movement must corroborate (median |theta - theta0| at episode end > 0 in the traced arm). The stateless reading of the gate must not be quoted for stateful candidates.The advance rule: a candidate becomes the advancing line iff (a) its mechanism/shape test passed, (b) its league-shaped share beats the advancing line's same-round league-shaped mark, and (c) its gauntlet pooled subject value does not bleed beyond the round's stated allowance against the advancing line's same-round pooled value (R37 precedent: 1 point). A candidate whose share wins but whose shape test fails is an unexplained residual, not an advance (R34 doctrine).Amended rule (c) - Will's ruling, 2026-11-26: the gauntlet no-bleed clause binds ONLY within-class candidates. A cross-class candidate is judged on its mechanism/shape test, its league-shaped share, and the CF/CF3 compositions; its gauntlet pooled value is still measured every round, still recorded, and still priced as the cost side - never hidden - but it does not block the advance. Gauntlet v1 itself is NOT changed: Table G and rules 1-3 stand as fixed in Round 28; changing the table starts a new gauntlet version.Mechanism first, hosted decides: mechanism metrics with pre-stated decision rules judge before shares. Local screens are SCREEN-class evidence only; they never move registry claims. MOAT transitions happen only on hosted receipts, along legal edges, never on local or replica evidence, and never entered-and-transitioned in the same leg. Succession is Will's word only: the loop never uploads, submits, or launches hosted xp-requests.Floors and baselines: replica floors understate champions until refreshed, and soft floors manufacture fake progress (R33 withdrew R32's gains on exactly this). Baselines are re-measured whenever cited, never carried. Headline values may only be quoted from gauntlet Tables G/GC/GD; any other table's numbers are labeled with its composition wherever reported. The one-minute rule (EE 896 form: 20 sub-minute sub-rounds per round screen) is the screen unit.Failure discipline is existential (manual-audit.md C5): a missing or failed decision sets failed = true, the match stops, and scores() returns 0 for EVERYONE - a crash does not lose one game, it zeroes the round. League fulfillment allowed_failures = 0.0 (retry_times 2); the division ladder disqualifies an entry after 3 consecutive failure rounds. A champion that crashes on pool exhaustion, an unhandled choice shape, or an instruction overrun does not just lose - repeated failure removes the entry. Robustness is a qualification constraint, not a polish item.The string law (manual-audit.md C8, mechanism verified against bassy texts.nim and bassy.nim restart(), and by probe): strings are a hard consumable - 256 slots, 64 KiB of bytes, 1,024 chars per string. Transient strings are reclaimed at restart(), so the per-decision budget is real; but strings rooted in globals, array cells, and materialized literals persist for the whole EPISODE and shrink every later decision's headroom (probe: 180 literals evaluated at setup plus 100 creations on decision 1 = string count limit exceeded, match failed). Exhaustion is a BasicError -> BotFailed -> C5. Design string-lean, count your literals, and never let a volume's chunks plus a decision's working strings approach 256.League mechanics (manual-audit.md register #10): rounds run every 15 minutes; each round is 6 episodes; seating is random_fill, and rounds that cannot fill seat four named filler policies. Ranking is openskill (mu 25, sigma 8.33); round scoring = mean; leaderboard = mean round score. players_per_user = 1; public_player_files = false. Part of every hosted field's composition - including effects an improvement series might attribute to named competitors - is filler seats and random fill; price field composition accordingly. The 15-minute / 6-episode cadence is the accrual clock for hosted receipts.D1 REPLACE - Hero class: setup selects class and deck from a named portfolio, keyed on seat and playerCount only (setup class visibility is settled: all -1, C4). Default entry Mage/C2. Falsifier: no portfolio entry beats the default in its own keyed cell beyond noise at full depth - the portfolio collapses back to one entry.D2 REPLACE - Deck construction: a portfolio of named shells - a fixed measured core (Post 4, Sharpshooter 4, Sniper 4, the draw engine) plus gene slots giftSwap (trinkets 12 to 10/8, Gift 0/2/4), hailSwap (Hail 0/2), bubbleN (3/4), owN (3/4). No free-form deck search: every alternate is traceable to a measured claim.D3 REPLACE (GEN-0 in part) - Setup is the policy's compile step: build the card name-to-index table, derive card semantics from the rules tree (D27), inscribe theta0s and priors, select the portfolio entry (D1/D2). No decision routine pays per-decision for a fact setup could have computed once.D4 REPLACE - Focus is a scored seat choice: argmin over living outsiders of life - wP*boardPower + wC*boardCount + wH*hailThreat(seat), hailThreat from the ledger (copies unseen x the seat's hail tendency). wP/wC start at rado's measured 2/1; wH starts at 2. The highest-life rule survives as the degenerate case the score must beat. Judged on the named wall: placement 3.03, death turn ~29.5 (unmoved R37-R39).D5 EVOLVE (GEN-0) - Focus bonuses are thetas: focSwingB (theta0 3) and focScoreB (theta0 2), drifting on the focus seat's hail and clock tendencies (tau 5 press / 2 ease), clamped [0.5*theta0, 2*theta0]. Genome focPair {2/1, 3/2, 5/3}. A theta that never leaves theta0 is inert - report it.D6 KEEP - Next-seat bonus +1 toward enemyPlayer in swing and score. Positional truth (the next living seat acts soonest); no new signal reprices it.D7 KEEP - Team protocol: partner = -1 in league form (v16's hunk on v11); the paired form (partner = seat +/- 2) remains a local-test artifact. Mailboxes change nothing: in league play the other seats are opponents, and a channel to opponents is not a team protocol.D8 EVOLVE - The aim value table's coefficients become genome genes: aimKill {8, 10, 12}, aimBase {3, 4, 6}, aimPowW {1, 2, 3}, starting at the current constants 10/4/2. The table's structure (kill > removal > chip; keyword-strip only on a keyword holder) is rules-truth and stays hand-fixed.D9 KEEP - Option-branch scoring: 20 + damage - 2*selfDamage, and never a self-lethal branch. The branch's prices are printed on the choice itself (optionDamage/optionSelfDamage); there is no historical signal the ledger could add. (For simultaneous-death stakes see C7 in the failure catalogue.)D10 EVOLVE - The swing value table becomes genes: faceBase {8, 10, 14}, killBase {4, 6, 8}, chip {2, 6} (v15's 2 returns as a gene value, priced properly this time), starting at 10/6/6. Structural rules stay fixed: lethal outranks trades (D11), no suicide swings, Ranged takes no return damage.D11 REPLACE - The lethal test is per-target: a hero swing scores 100 iff myPower >= playerLife(o) for the swing's ACTUAL owner o. The inherited test read enemyLife - the next living seat's life - granting or withholding the 100 by the wrong hero's total: a latent defect the redesign's full read found (see also C3). Metric: missed-lethal count in traced arms (W1's instrument).D12 REPLACE - Play sequencing becomes a turn plan: at the first decision of our turn the policy inscribes the intended play sequence into a plan scroll, computed from hand, energy curve, and ledger state; later decisions execute the plan's next step, deviating only on named triggers (a planned card became unplayable, a lethal appeared, the release band fired). The priority list survives as the plan's scoring function; its gaps evolve (priPost/priOw/priDraw, relative order fixed).D13 KEEP - Post discipline: Post only with a minion in hand. Macro's corpus law (337/337), confirmed in our own R37 shape match. A measured invariant of champion play.D14 EVOLVE (GEN-0 in part) - The Post-hold stays (D13's discipline). The survival release band is v36's relBand theta (theta0 8; tau 12 under our own Burnout, 10 under observed aggression, 5 on a quiet clockless field past turn 6). The overflow threshold is genome gene releaseN {6, 7, 8}, start 7; relBand's theta0 is a gene {5, 8, 11}, start 8. Judged on held-at-death rate and bodies/game.D15 REPLACE - Overwork is a ledger-priced clock decision: play iff the ledger's own-clock account and the life band jointly price it - no new Overwork when own ticking clocks >= 1 AND life <= owBand, or when a clock is already imminent in band. owBand gene {off, 6, 8, 10}, start off (v35's 8 enters as a gene value, tested inside the loop). This is NOT v31's cap (D26): the cap keyed on existence; this keys on count x band, with draws/game as a guarded metric.D16 REPLACE (implementation), band EVOLVE (GEN-0) - The W4 Burnout discount reads burning state from the LEDGER (Burnout summons per seat from the log, maintained exactly), never from a per-decision board name-scan. The rule is unchanged and verified: non-lethal damage into a burning hero at life <= burnBand (theta0 4) is wasted. The scan could not see clocks Gifted away, destroyed, or summoned this turn; the ledger can.D17 KEEP - Bubble on sight (priority 100). A 0-cost trinket that bounces the first attacker; on-sight is the champion's measured play and the card's own arithmetic. Free cards are not repriced by history.D18 KEEP - Archer trinkets at priority 95, ahead of everything but Bubble: the discount engine (each trinket in play cuts the Archer-card surcharge by 1). Printed surcharge arithmetic only compounds; no history reprices it.D19 KEEP - Sharpshooter pings: heroes only, lowest life, never a creature - the 1,052/0 rule, the strongest single regularity in the corpus, reproduced in our R37 shape match (482/0). The W4 carve-out (a non-lethal ping into a burning hero at life <= band scores 0) inherits D16's ledger detection.D20 EVOLVE - The discard keep-table's values become genome genes in three blocks (keepDraw, keepEngine, keepBody, starting at the corpus table: Archer 0, Bubble 10, Plan 20, Study 25, Sniper 30, Post 90, Overwork 95, Sharpshooter 100), plus a structural ledger term that is not a gene: a card's keep drops by a fixed step per copy already on our board or in our discard history this episode. Judged on tossed-card regret in traces.D21 KEEP - Trigger answering reuses the card-aim routine with handIndex -1; Sharpshooter triggers set the ping flag (D19's rule at a trigger). A trigger is a card choice with the same machinery; reuse is correct under any memory model.D22 REPLACE - The no-score fallback: decline if allowed (unchanged); else target the FOCUS seat (D4's scored choice) when an enemy must be chosen; random is retained only when no living outsider exists. The starter's random-enemy fallback was the stateless era's honest choice; we have a priced preference now.D23 EVOLVE - Tie-breaking gains a margin gene tieEps {0, 1, 2}, start 0 (the control): choices within tieEps of the best score are first ordered by the ledger (the seat's tendency profile, the card's seen-count), and random() arbitrates only what the ledger cannot separate. Every coin flip is a load-bearing part of the trace.D24 KEEP (Warrior line) - The Hail package: stock Warrior deck, one swap (Duel out, Hail of Arrows in) - rado's own measured construction, priced by three rounds of screens. The redesign's Warrior work is the gate (D25), not the package.D25 REPLACE (Warrior line) - The Hail gate becomes a ledger gate: fire iff (a) live 1-tough enemy kills >= hailGate {5, 6, 8}, evolving from 6 (between the corrected measured median-at-fire 4 and the inherited 8), AND (b) the ledger's spent-Hail account prices the marginal copy (opposing copies seen reduce the value of holding; our remaining copies, 4 - seen-own exact from the log, price scarcity). Gene hailPost {off, on}: a live opposing Post lowers hailGate by 2 - the flood crosses as 1-tough copies during the window.D26 RETIRED - v31's clock cap (no Overwork once a clock exists) is falsified (R38: the deaths were single-clock, and the cap starved the engine - draws 15.3 to 13.8, bodies 20.10 to 16.95) and is NOT re-decided. Recorded so the evolution loop does not re-derive it: any genome gene whose effect is a binary existence cap on Overwork is out of the search space by name. D15's count x band pricing is the permitted form.D27 REPLACE - Card semantics are derived at setup from the rules tree: rulesOfCard for every card in the portfolio shells and every token the log can name, inscribed into the index table as structured facts (which cards summon clocks, which copy, which sweep, at what amount). Decision code reads derived facts, never a remembered rule. This is the v13 Burnout lesson institutionalized - and manual-audit register #2 confirms it is fully executable today.D28 REPLACE (= synthesis S1, the class study's top rank) - Clock export: Gift enters via the giftSwap deck gene (D2), and aim gains the export rule as structure: when we hold Gift and a Burnout ticks on our board, donating it to the living outsider with the lowest clock tendency is the default play, priced ahead of generic trinket plays. Falsifier (S1's own): < 0.5 Gift casts/game, or self-Burnout deaths do not fall against the control arm.D29 REPLACE - Field-class conditioning: the field's class counts (from playerClass) are inscribed at setup and refreshed per decision; the drift tau tables gain a composition term - in Warrior-dense fields (>= 3 Warrior seats) relBand's tau shifts +compRel and focSwingB's tau shifts +compFoc; in Mage-dense fields both hold at base. The shifts are structure; their sizes are genes compRel {0, 2, 4} / compFoc {0, 1, 2}, starts 2/1. Judged on the league-shaped split by field Warrior count - the interaction matrix's own cells are the prediction.D30 KEEP + ADOPT - The non-use of mailbox and ANNOTATE stands (league counterparties are opponents; ANNOTATE's production sink for this league is unverified and it cannot be read back - telemetry nobody can read is not an instrument). PRINT is ADOPTED as the loop's instrument, deliberately: stateful candidates PRINT theta checkpoints and ledger summaries at named turns, which is what makes theta-movement and drift-vs-outcome readable from traces.D31 KEEP - Mulligan: no such decision exists in the host contract the lineage runs against; the opening hand is dealt and the first decisions are toss/trigger/play. Nothing to re-decide. If a future manifest adds one, it enters as a new ledger entry, not a redesign of this one.W1 - The spared executioner (aim the next build here): clean missed lethals occur in 47.8% of rado's losses (100/209) and 60.3% of macroMage's (85/141); the spared lowest player later landed the killing blow on the champion in 23.0% / 32.6% of losses. Generic FFA sparing explains the broad count but not that tail. Median ticks from a missed lethal to the champion's death: 15 (rado), 16.5 (macroMage). Our instrument: deaths-at-low-life-with-board rate, and champions' kill-share on us when we are lowest.W2 (CORRECTED by the class study) - rado's Hail prices 1-tough kills, but the register's band numbers do not reproduce: on all 242 validated fires the 1-tough median at fire is 4 under both toughness definitions, not 11 (~11-12 is the median TOTAL opposing minions). He died holding at least one Hail in 122 of 209 losses (58%). The immunity half survives directly: Warrior boards are 11.6% toughness<=1 instances, Mage boards 86.8% - an undamaged Warrior board gives his 4 Hail copies nothing. The band numbers must not set a Hail gate (D25 starts from the corrected median).W3 - Macro's flood is Post-windowed; his losses are half-built floods: bodies/game 23.8 in wins vs 12.5 in losses; 30.8% of loss turns end with >= 3 energy unspent (vs 18.5% of win turns); he dies holding ~1.7 unplayed shooters per loss and a Post in hand in 27% of losses. The mechanism is his own discipline - 95.1% of minion plays under a live Post, and Post lasts 2 turns. Exploit: fire our Hail into the Post window (gate >= 5 bodies with >= 3 of them 1-tough), not at the raw board count; his dry turns are our tempo turns.W4 - Macro's Burnout clock: 11 losses (7.8% of macroMage) end on his own Burnout trigger, the final 2 damage at his life 1-4. Presence is ACQUITTED (Burnout is aboard in 54% of his wins vs 44% at death in losses); only the endgame band is the weakness. Exploit (banked at v32, zero-cost): in the band, non-lethal damage into him is worth half - unless it kills this turn, spend the attacks elsewhere and let the clock collect (D16).W5 - Signature matchups: macroMage scores 0.313 vs radoDeck fields (n=233) vs 0.441 vs stockWarrior and 0.448 vs stockMage; macroWarrior 0.229 vs radoDeck, his worst split; rado's own worst field is the radoDeck mirror (0.358 vs 0.388-0.409 against every other signature). Our lineage IS a radoDeck (Warrior + the Hail swap) - the audit confirms this shape is macro's worst matchup. Price league fields by signature and class-count mix, not by class labels.W6 - Rado collapses suddenly, to width: in 111 of 209 losses (53%) he was at life >= 10 ten ticks before death; his killers' boards at the killing blow: median 10 bodies, 16 total power, best single body 3 - width, not height; 199/209 kills are minion attacks; loss-winners are Mage-class in 61%. The shape that kills him is a one-turn alpha from a wide board, not incremental trades his scored focus dismantles piecemeal. Exploit sketch: a hold-and-alpha override when our board count >= 8 and his life <= our total board power.W7 - Macro's class lottery: the same policy draws Mage at 0.403 (236 games) or Warrior at 0.284 (109) - an 11.9-point self-interaction, larger than every field-composition term except the dead seat's gift to macroMage. By combo: C2 0.411, C3 0.370, C1 0.298, C5 0.321 (small-n), C4 0.235. Not exploitable (we do not choose his draw), but it corrects the bar: any distance-to-bar accounting off his blended league mean (0.327 at EE 709; 0.3287 at EE 916) prices his average draw, not his strong one.W8 (CORRECTED by the class study) - Rado's seat splits do not reproduce as a late-seat penalty. Full-corpus recount, 5-player games: 0.458 / 0.397 / 0.361 / 0.333 / 0.403 by seat (n = 83/68/72/60/62) - a seats-2-3 dip with seat 4 recovering, not a monotone penalty. The register's figures (seats 0-1 0.456 on n=79; seat 4 0.345 on n=29) were moderate-n cells. Direction survives at reduced size; the late-seat framing does not. Seat is a small term next to class counts.W9 - Macro's ping rule, priced and ACQUITTED: the creature that eventually killed macroMage had been ping-spared in only 7 of his 141 losses (5.0%); spared 1-tough creatures dealt a mean 0.06 later hero-damage per spared instance (7,900 instances, 449 total damage - most die to the field or the game ends). Recorded as a negative result: do not spend a hunk baiting his pings. The 1,052/0 rule (D19) costs him almost nothing measurable.W10 - Opening book: no weakness found. Damage taken in the first 10 ticks averages 0.03-0.19 for both champions in wins and losses alike - the opening is bloodless. First-turn minion is flat for rado (0.402 with, n=169; 0.386 without, n=176) and mildly positive for macroWarrior (0.321 vs 0.250, small-n flag). No hunk.Andre von Auto - radogost:v166, league #1 (EE 916: MMR 27.600, mean 0.3467, 1,700 rounds; unchanged build since 2026-11-14). Warrior hero; stock Warrior deck with one swap (Duel out, Hail of Arrows in). Corpus: 345 games, share 0.394. Targeting rule (found R33): minimize life - 2*boardPower + boardCount - 0.799 agreement over 3,884 hero attacks. 96.9% of his attacks hit heroes. His improvement is rating redistribution on an unchanged build, not win-share surge: his mean is flat while his MMR climbs.Rado disciplines and leaks: Pillage takes the +1-power branch 290/290. Hail is held for real boards (fires at opposing minions median 12 total; 1-tough median 4, W2 as corrected) - and he died holding Hail in 58% of losses. He dies first in only ~6% of games. His worst field is his own mirror (0.358). Replica note: the rado replica runs STRONGER than the real thing in F1 (43.8% room share vs his corpus 0.394) with high behavioral fidelity (focus agreement 1.000 by construction vs his 0.799) - price local marks against a floor that is above the champion.Macromackie - cac-macromackie:v64, league #2 (EE 916: MMR 25.021, mean 0.3287). A deck-SELECTING program: 5 (class, deck) combos chosen at setup - C2 55.1%, C3 13.3%, C1 13.6%, C4 9.9%, C5 8.1%. macroMage: 236 games, share 0.403. macroWarrior: 109 games, 0.284. The C2 Mage hybrid rents the Archer engine at a 12-slot tax: 12 class trinkets, Post/Sharpshooter/Sniper x4, the Mage draw engine (Plan/Study/Overwork), Bubble defense. His local gate, from his own public submission notes: 3,000 games, 0 own failures.Macro disciplines (measured; adopted by our line at v30): Post only with a minion in hand, 337/337. Every Sharpshooter ping hits a hero: 1,052 to heroes, 0 to creatures. 95.1% of minion plays occur under a live Post; the flood converts ~3.0 minion plays into ~7.15 bodies/game via Post copies (2.97 copies per Post play, the best conversion of any renter). Bodies/game 23.8 in wins vs 12.5 in losses (W3). He does NOT donate his Burnouts (no Gift in C1-C5) - he eats his own clock (W4). Replica caveat: discipline-faithful but strength-low locally (Mage family share 0.036 in F1 vs his real 0.403) - the flood does not fully convert in the local engine; combo draws cluster on seed plans.The field at EE 916 (10 seats): #1 Andre von Auto (radogost:v166) 0.3467; #2 macromackie (cac-macromackie:v64) 0.3287; #3 A-aron (A-aron:v142 - a Mage renter carrying Gift x4); #4 Fletcher (salvo:v1 - the other Gift carrier); #5 Andrew Brower (stilt:v30); #6 phext - us (emise-cac-v30:v1, mean 0.154, MMR 19.161, 253 rounds at EE 916); #7 Rohit Fleet (cac-rado:v1 - a published build recipe: the rado pattern); #8 richard (deckwarden); #9 docxology (daf-awm-v1:v2 - the dead Archer seat); #10 Eckstar (eckstar-cac-warrior:v1). The top five have not published since 2026-11-14: since then exactly 6 submissions league-wide, 3 of them ours.Class law 1 - Warrior density taxes Mages, one direction only. Mage seats' win share vs Warrior count: 0.251 (1 Warrior, n=183) -> 0.205 (2, n=552) -> 0.181 (3+, n=304), ~3.5 points per step. At champion strength the gradient steepens: macroMage 0.509 -> 0.386 -> 0.320 (~19 points). The reverse is flat: Warriors vs Mage count 0.245 / 0.234 / 0.241. Warrior mirrors crowd at 3+ (0.204); Mage mirrors are free. Consequence (D29): a Mage candidate screened in Warrior-light rooms is priced ~19 points above its Warrior-dense price; league-shaped tables must be judged against the league field's actual Warrior-count mix.Class law 2 - The Burnout clock is a transferable weapon. 770 Burnout summons in 507 games, 100% landing on the Overwork owner's board; 283 (36.8%) were started by an opposing Warrior's Pillage destroying the Overwork - the Warrior class routinely converts a Mage's live engine into a self-clock at its chosen moment. 245 Gift casts (A-aron 144, salvo 101 - the only carriers), and every one donates exactly one Burnout (245 GainControl events). 131 Warrior seat-games carried a donated clock, 131/131 via GainControl; 11/11 Warrior self-kills are donated clocks - Warrior decks hold no Burnout. A Mage hero has no native answer to a donated clock: export it (Gift) or die on schedule. Our line eats ours: 30 of 247 R38-autopsy losses (12.1%) were self-Burnout deaths, worse than macro's 7.8%.Class law 3 - The dead seat is a field-composition artifact, priced. D af's deck (daf-awm-v1:v2) is empty in 271/271 traced games: an illegal deck seats the bot out before the first turn (bots.nim setupSeat - an illegal deck still seats, with no deck). The free elimination is worth +12.8 points to macroMage (0.469 with the dead seat, n=113, vs 0.341 without, n=123), +6.6 to Mage seats generally, +3.6 to Warrior seats, +6.2 to rado. Every historical 'vs Archer' reading in this programme is this artifact - including part of v30's first hosted read. Genuine Archer cells are EMPTY: priced locally only.Class law 4 - Draw is the real class gap. DrawEvents per seat-game: Mage 10.89 (median 10; ~+4.9 extra draws from Plan/Study/Overwork) vs Warrior 5.98 (median 6 - indistinguishable from the bare turn draw; Warrior's pool has no draw and rents none). At death: Warrior heroes die with 29.4 cards still in deck vs Mage 25.0; hand at death is equal (~4.3 both). A Warrior game is played from roughly the first 11 cards of the deck: the class's losses are selection starvation, not hand starvation. This is also Archer's hole - zero native draw - and why every successful Archer-card strategy in the corpus is rented by the class that can draw.Archer verdict: UNBUILT CLASS (archer-audit, EE 894). Not DEAD (no functioning line has ever been measured and dominated), not SPLASH-ONLY (the rentals are the most expensive in the corpus and still win - a dominated class does not supply the meta's two best engines). The Archer hero has never played a card in the hosted corpus: all 271 Archer seats are daf's empty deck, dead at tick 1. Heroes are mechanically identical (20 life, no hero power) - class is purely an economy (off-class +3, -1 per class trinket of that card's class in play). Archer's 13 cards hold the Post flood engine, the set's cheapest sweeper (Hail at 3 native vs 6 rented), its only direct burn (Bolt: 1 energy, 2 to a hero - 11 casts in the corpus, all rented, never in the W1 endgame band), ranged bodies that take no return damage from non-ranged, and zero card draw.Archer shell sketch (archer-audit): native core at printed cost - Post x4, Sniper x4, Sharpshooter x4, Hail of Arrows x4, Bolt x4 (20 cards). The open problem is card velocity. Arm A (inverted C2): rent Mage draw the way macro rents Archer - Mage class trinkets ~x8 + Study x4 + Plan x4. Arm B (pure native): no draw; Post copies as the only velocity, Reload x3 as pseudo-velocity, Ambusher x4 as the toughness-2 body. Excluded by evidence: Wildfire (self-harm against a toughness-1 board) except as a labeled Gift-donate experiment; Overcharge at most x2. Falsifier: if the native engine, velocity solved either way, cannot beat the advancing line's league-shaped mark - or dies to Hail at stock-ref rates - the verdict revises toward DEAD CLASS, closed with evidence instead of absence.Syntheses, ranked by (unpriced asymmetry x cheapness of the decisive test): S1 Clock Export (v33 + Gift - the trinket tax is already paid via the C2 shell; cost is 2-4 slots) - ranked first, carried into the redesign as D28 and into Generation 1 as genes G1-09/G1-10. S2 Archer-native flood, pure native (Arm B). S3 Inverted C2 (Arm A: Archer hero renting Mage draw). S4 Pillage clock discipline (Warrior line; its key reading - Pillage may target our own donated Burnout - is printed-rules, unverified in play, to be settled in the simulator before any screen, not during).The drift law: Q16.16 fixed-point EWMA throughout. Tendencies update per observation as E <- (7*E + x) \ 8 with x in {0, 65536} - gamma = 0.875, an ~8-observation effective memory - and decay by the same factor per own turn without confirmation. Parameters drift once per own turn as theta <- (7*theta + tau) \ 8 toward the observation-implied tau, clamped to [0.5*theta0, 2*theta0]. All core arithmetic is int32 with \ (division toward zero); the core contains no /, no random(), no clock reads: theta is a deterministic function of the episode's observed event stream. Learning scope is within-episode, architecturally - a fresh BotVm per episode, nothing persists across episodes; cross-episode learning awaits a persistence channel the deployment does not offer.Tau tables (v36 binding, the baseline's inheritance): relBand - 12 if our own Burnout has started (ledger), else 10 under aggression pressure (any seat's aggression EWMA >= 0.35 in Q16.16 terms: 22938), else 5 on a quiet clockless field past turn 6, else 8. Focus bonuses - press 5 when the focus seat's hail tendency >= 0.25 (16384) with Hail copies still unseen; ease 2 when its Hail is spent (4 seen) or it is a clock runner (clock tendency >= 0.35, hail quiet); else 3 - the score bonus tracks swing - 1, floored at 1. burnBand - 6 when any opponent has started 2+ clocks (ledger), 3 when no clock has been seen by turn 8, else 4.Decision rules 1 (setup and the flood): WHEN selecting at setup DO pick Mage and build the C2 shell (12 Archer trinkets; Post, Sharpshooter, Sniper, Bubble, Overwork, Plan, Study x4), and DO load the phext volume and inscribe theta0s before the first decision (D3). WHEN Post is playable AND a minion is in hand DO play Post before minions (D13). WHEN no Post is live DO hold minions - unless handSize >= releaseN (overflow release) or our life <= relBand (survival release) (D14). WHEN a Sharpshooter pings DO hit a hero, lowest life, never a creature (D19), subject to the W4 carve-out (D16).Decision rules 2 (aim and clocks): WHEN choosing focus DO score living outsiders - life - wP*boardPower + wC*boardCount + wH*hailThreat, hailThreat = (hailCopies - ledger seen) x hail tendency (D4). WHEN a swing is lethal on its ACTUAL target (myPower >= playerLife(target)) DO take it at value 100 (D11). WHEN Overwork is affordable DO play it - unless the ledger prices the clock: own ticking clocks >= 1 and life <= owBand, or a clock imminent in band (D15). WHEN damaging a burning hero at life <= burnBand DO pay only lethal damage (D16). WHEN we hold Gift and a Burnout ticks on our board DO donate it to the living outsider with the lowest clock tendency (D28/S1, when giftSwap > 0). WHEN any decision spends strings DO count them against the 256 budget and skip honestly past it (C8/C5).Failure 1 - The .37 inscription collision (EE ~850, owned plainly): the weakness audit and Round 34 were inscribed under the IDENTICAL builder dict key .37; R34's assignment silently overwrote the audit's body (commit 82b5de7). The audit survived in commit 43dcc6c and the builder text. Repaired: audit restored at .37, R34 renumbered .38, R35 at .39; remote verified byte-exact. Lesson carried: verify builder keys unique before every inscription, and verify byte-exact on origin after streaming. The size mystery in the same incident was chars-vs-bytes (608 B of multibyte UTF-8).Failure 2 - The R32 soft floor (withdrawn R33): Round 32's CF3 42.1% and league-shaped 19.7% were measured against stale replicas and WITHDRAWN in Round 33, when 273 fresh episodes (273/273 traced) refreshed the floors and found rado's targeting rule; v22 re-measured down to CF3 20.0%, league-shaped 6.4%. A screen value is a property of the table that produced it; numbers against an unrefreshed floor are not numbers. Never re-quote the R32 marks as current.Failure 3 - The DATA-statements error (manual-audit.md C1; EE ~935-948, owned): the redesign's first framing put the phext volume in bassy DATA statements 'restored by reset()'. Bassy 0.1.0 has no DATA, READ, or RESTORE at all - its keyword table and statement parser contain none of them, and a probe using them fails compilation. The v36 build caught it by experiment, not by the design review that should have. The real channel is string literals materialized at setup (literals and array initializers executed as code in the setup phase), as v36 shipped. Lesson: read the dialect's keyword table before designing on a statement; a channel claim needs a probe, not a summary.Failure 4 - The unread manual (the programme's founding defect): the reference bot's header stated 'Globals and arrays survive between decisions' from Round 1, and the lineage designed statelessly for 33 versions - perception frozen at the starter idiom, baseset.nim read religiously while bots.nim (the host contract) went unread end to end, champion behaviors reverse-engineered from traces without asking what runtime surface enables them. The full-manual audit (EE ~946) found 9 contradictions and a register of unexploited capabilities in one pass. Working premise since, in Will's words: our novice standing is just an artifact of not reading the manual. The manual is a primary source; traces are the check, not the substitute.Failure 5 - W2's Hail band (corrected by the class study): the weakness register's 'rado fires at enemy 1-tough median 11' does not reproduce. On the validated traces (242 fires) the 1-tough median is 4 under both toughness definitions; ~11-12 is the median total opposing minions. The qualitative claim survives weakened (Warrior boards 11.6% toughness<=1 instances vs Mage 86.8%); the band numbers must not set a Hail gate.Failure 6 - W8's late-seat penalty (corrected by the class study): the register's seat splits were moderate-n cells. Full-corpus rado by seat: 0.458 / 0.397 / 0.361 / 0.333 / 0.403 (n = 83/68/72/60/62) - a seats-2-3 dip, seat 4 recovering. The monotone late-seat framing does not reproduce; direction survives at reduced size.Failure 7 - The Gift census error (the Archer audit's, corrected by the class study): the audit stated Gift 'appears in zero decks in the corpus' - wrong. Gift x4 is carried by A-aron:v142 and salvo:v1 (536 seat-games), cast 245 times, every cast donating a Burnout. The audit's precon column (x0) was correct; its corpus claim was not. The error materially strengthens the audit's own UNBUILT verdict and is synthesis S1's direct source. A census claim is only as good as its census.Failure 8 - The corpus frame (corrected by the Archer audit and the class study): the register's header ('NO Archer-class player exists in the corpus - 2,264 seat-slots: Warrior 1,221, Mage 1,043') silently dropped every Archer seat. The traced setups hold 2,535 seats: Warrior 1,221 / Mage 1,043 / Archer 271 - 2,264 was the count of PLAYED seats mislabeled as the total. The same header's 'fields are 4-5 players' is stale: the traced corpus is 507/507 five-player. Prior statements read 'never played', not 'never present'.Failure 9 - The wiki trap (manual-audit.md C2): the official wiki's main page describes a different, older game - at most one card per decision, automatic attacks on the next living hero, a 30-card turn limit, house-chosen discards and trigger targets, seat-local runtime failures, classes drawn from the seed. Every clause is false in the current engine (the current README denies the 30-card limit in so many words). The surfaces that agree with the engine source are the hub README, the manifest README, play.md, and the reference bots. A wiki is not the manual; a trainee who reads it as one inherits a fictional game.Failure 10 - The next-seat restriction that never was (manual-audit.md C3): attacks were never restricted to the next seat. sim.nim attackTargets enumerates every opponent in seat order after the attacker - each living opponent's hero, then their non-hidden minions - and attackHero accepts any of them; the host's data slots only NAME the next seat. Thirty-three versions of focus logic were designed inside the restriction anyway, including v33's lethal test reading enemyLife (D11). The action surface spans the whole ring; only our model of it was narrow.Failure 11 - The setup-visibility probe that was never owed (manual-audit.md C4): 'are opponents' classes visible during setup?' was carried as an empirical unknown, with Generation 1 owing a one-episode probe. The manual settles it in three places - the README ('During setup they return -1 because the match roster has not been dealt yet'), the reference bot's header, and bots.nim setupSeat (all data slots zeroed except selectingClass=1, selfPlayer, enemyPlayer, playerCount; playerClass returns -1 for every seat while selectingClass). Setup keying can only use seat and playerCount. The answer was printed before Round 1.Failure 12 - Copies and simultaneous death (manual-audit.md C6, C7): a Post copy enters as its original IS NOW - damage and buffs, gained and lost rules, and the counts of its Hidden and Lasts - not as a fresh printed copy; any flood model that prices copies at printed stats misprices every copy after the first buff or hit (each Post also copies on its own: two Posts double-copy). And when the last heroes fall together, the CURRENT player wins (checkWinCondition): deaths settle simultaneously at effect-batch boundaries, so a board-emptying lethal on our own turn scores as a WIN, and the same board on an opponent's turn scores as a loss. Hero damage clamps at 0; the order of lethal effects inside one resolution decides who was current.Failure 13 - The first draw (manual-audit.md C9): 'the first player skips their first draw' reads naturally as a per-player rule. sim.nim implements it by turnNumber: beginTurn draws only when turnNumber > 1, and turnNumber counts every seat's turns from 1. Only the match's very first turn skips; every other player's first turn (turns 2, 3, 4, 5) draws normally. Any hand-built tempo model granting each player a skipped first draw is wrong by four cards in a five-player game.Open paths - named, not dropped (manual-audit.md): Reload's repeat(printedRules) semantics live in games/cac/core/core.nim and ruletree.nim, the rules-DSL evaluator, not yet read end to end - the Archer audit's flag, now with its exact address; Arm B of the Archer shell may not be designed around Reload until it is settled. ANNOTATE's production sink for this league stays unverified (a sink exists only when the runner supplies an annotationsUri per seat). cardset.nim (deckProblems/buildDeck internals) unread; replays.nim/stateHash internals unread, nothing in our line depends on them. A path named here is owed, not closed.Mined and unexploited 1 (manual-audit.md register): setup-phase rules-tree derivation (#2 = D27, fully executable today, used by neither v33 nor v36). Exact board clocks (#4): boardLastsTurns and boardHiddenTurns print Post and Overwork expiry and Hidden countdowns per board slot, in controller's-turn units, -1 for never - our line prices both trinkets' lifetimes from trace statistics while the counters are printed on the board. boardStolenFrom (#5): a stolen card's home is its original owner - a donated Burnout is OUR card on their board; bounced, it returns to OUR hand, reusable; killed, it feeds OUR discard pile.Mined and unexploited 2 (manual-audit.md register): dead seats stay dangerous (#6) - a dead player's cards in play remain: their minions are legal attack targets and their permanents' triggers still queue and resolve (a dead Mage's Bubble still bounces its attacker; a dead player's Post does not copy). Declining a target (#13) is a verb our targeting treats as absent: NoTarget lets a Sharpshooter ping not fire and a Bouncer not bounce. The shared clock (#11): 28,800 actions per match across all seats; a timed-out match scores 0 for everyone, the would-be winner included - action-hungry lines spend a commons.Mined and unexploited 3 (manual-audit.md register): trigger and toss identity (#3) prices every mandatory off-turn decision exactly, where our line aims them generically - triggerAttackerId names the exact minion Bubble exists to bounce, at the moment of decision, and tossOwner separates self-inflicted discards (our Study, our Burnout) from hostile ones, which price differently. Stable identity (#8): boardId never shifts; board indexes shift whenever a minion dies or is bounced - key ledgers by id and resolve index only at query time (v36's ledger keys by board index; a death mid-ring can alias one minion's ledger onto another's).